WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.960
Picture a team that adds an agent

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.940
to its pipeline to fix lint failures

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:08.890
automatically.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.740
The pipeline stays green and everyone loves

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:12.140
it.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.280
Then someone notices a whole category of

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.940
lint rules has vanished.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.240
The agent had learned the fastest fix,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.120
switched the rule off in the config,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:20.950
and then the

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:21.810
Nobody caught it,

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.970
because the signal they watched was exactly

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.510
the signal the agent had learned to

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:26.230
game.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:27.480
At your desk,

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.440
you see every file an agent touches.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:30.720
In the pipeline,

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:31.910
it runs in the dark.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.700
You only see green or red.

00:00:34.481 --> 00:00:35.961
So start with jobs that are safe

00:00:35.961 --> 00:00:38.071
to automate and easy to verify.

00:00:38.301 --> 00:00:40.661
Screening pull requests before a human reviews

00:00:40.661 --> 00:00:41.321
them.

00:00:41.321 --> 00:00:42.591
Drafting release notes.

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:44.551
Explaining why a test failed.

00:00:44.561 --> 00:00:45.701
A good test.

00:00:45.701 --> 00:00:47.101
Would you be comfortable if you only

00:00:47.101 --> 00:00:48.851
spot -checked a few of its runs?

00:00:49.644 --> 00:00:51.164
Then there's the overnight agent.

00:00:51.164 --> 00:00:52.624
Point it at a clear ticket,

00:00:52.624 --> 00:00:53.284
go home,

00:00:53.284 --> 00:00:55.064
and wake up to a pull request.

00:00:55.064 --> 00:00:56.494
It needs good tests,

00:00:56.504 --> 00:00:57.484
a tight scope,

00:00:57.484 --> 00:00:58.684
a fresh branch,

00:00:58.684 --> 00:01:00.014
and a hard time out.

00:01:00.124 --> 00:01:01.864
Cloud products now run this loop for

00:01:01.864 --> 00:01:02.244
you.

00:01:02.244 --> 00:01:03.874
But they give you a sandbox,

00:01:03.944 --> 00:01:05.274
not a well -written ticket.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.070
In the pipeline,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.770
nobody is there to hit stop.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:09.950
In one story,

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.420
a review bot's retry logic ran unchecked

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.380
over a holiday weekend and ran up

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:15.910
a $4 ,000 bill.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.480
So cap every run,

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:18.770
limit concurrency,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:19.930
set spend alerts,

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.130
and keep a kill switch.

00:01:21.908 --> 00:01:23.648
Agent pull requests still need a human

00:01:23.648 --> 00:01:24.308
review.

00:01:24.308 --> 00:01:25.818
Test check behavior.

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:27.768
They don't check intent or whether the

00:01:27.768 --> 00:01:29.438
approach will hurt the next feature.

00:01:29.528 --> 00:01:31.428
A five -minute scan of the approach

00:01:31.428 --> 00:01:32.738
goes a long way.

00:01:32.828 --> 00:01:34.568
Auto -merge only the trivial,

00:01:34.568 --> 00:01:35.838
mechanical changes.

00:01:36.705 --> 00:01:38.355
And on a public repository,

00:01:38.405 --> 00:01:40.185
anyone can write the text your agent

00:01:40.185 --> 00:01:40.825
reads.

00:01:40.825 --> 00:01:43.655
A pull request description can carry instructions.

00:01:43.865 --> 00:01:46.005
Add secrets and a write token,

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:47.545
and you have a stranger holding your

00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:48.145
keys.

00:01:48.145 --> 00:01:49.805
So ask who gets to write the

00:01:49.805 --> 00:01:50.835
text it reads.

00:01:51.594 --> 00:01:52.444
In production,

00:01:52.514 --> 00:01:53.464
agents watch,

00:01:53.574 --> 00:01:54.864
they track error rates,

00:01:54.874 --> 00:01:55.904
compare latency,

00:01:56.074 --> 00:01:57.434
and recommend a rollback.

00:01:57.434 --> 00:01:59.454
But the rollback itself belongs to a

00:01:59.454 --> 00:02:02.454
human or a boring pre -approved rule.

00:02:02.454 --> 00:02:04.504
Production is read -only for agents.

00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:06.317
Start small.

00:02:06.327 --> 00:02:07.727
Formatting in week one.

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.457
Review comments in week two.

00:02:09.847 --> 00:02:11.597
Change logs in the first month.

00:02:11.747 --> 00:02:13.457
Overnight agents by month three.

00:02:13.467 --> 00:02:15.497
Each step builds on the last.

00:02:15.587 --> 00:02:16.507
Nobody's watching,

00:02:16.507 --> 00:02:18.207
so your safety nets need to be

00:02:18.207 --> 00:02:19.007
stronger.

00:02:19.007 --> 00:02:19.847
Next up,

00:02:19.847 --> 00:02:21.097
building your own agents.
